WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.360
Film Courage: How important is screenplay
structure to your overall writing?

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.870
Is there a specific structure you try to follow?

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:13.030
Jeffrey Reddick: I kind of learned writing
through a ton of scripts at New Line [Cinema],

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:15.030
when I was there I would read scripts all
the time.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:20.710
I find that most of my stuff kind of falls
into the traditional three-act structure,

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:23.480
[Author Blake Snyder’s book] Save-The-Cat
thing where there’s an inciting incident.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.210
In don’t get into…there’s just some
stuff…I shouldn’t roll my eyes at it because

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:28.210
people do.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:32.890
But you know there are some books where they’ve
broken it down to the micro-science and for

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:34.390
me it’s just like too much.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:39.480
In the way, you don’t want to beat the creativity
out of somebody by giving them too much of

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.480
a structure.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.330
That’s why I think the three-act structure
is enough.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:47.290
Yes, at the end of your second act, something’s
got to like propel you into the third act

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:52.320
and things have got to get more intense and
more intense to your finale.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.490
I have a lot of friends who went to film school
and their one thing is like “I wish I’d

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.810
spent that money to make a film.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.160
I would have learned a lot more.”

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.990
It’s not anything against going to film
school but if you’re trying to teach somebody

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:13.049
who is artistic how to…you can teach them
how to turn that into a format that is going

00:01:13.049 --> 00:01:15.610
to be more palpable to the masses.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:20.130
But if you start to get too much into you
know, on page 20 this needs to happen, on

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:25.119
page 30 this has to happen, then you are stifling
people’s creativity in a way.

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:27.340
There is a reason that there’s a structure
like that.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.459
I’ve had friends give me a script and the
first draft is like 140 pages.

00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:34.229
I’m like “I’m not reading this.”

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:37.439
This isn’t a script, it’s a miniseries.

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:42.369
If they can tell the Titanic in 120 pages,
I think you can tell your story about a woman

00:01:42.369 --> 00:01:48.590
who is trying to find true love in her small
town in less than 140 pages.

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:51.740
There is a wisdom to the basic structure.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.070
When I sit down to write something, usually
I start with a concept.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:58.479
I’m very concept driven because I think
working at a studio for so long, I know the

00:01:58.479 --> 00:02:05.899
concept is going to get people’s attention
or not get it, so I start with using a concept

00:02:05.899 --> 00:02:11.640
and then I think of some scares as well and
then start building a story around a concept

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.640
is how I approach it.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.140
I know a lot of people start from characters
and some people start from a story.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:21.700
Everybody’s got their own personal kind
of creative style and how they work.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.070
For me I always start with the concept of
the story.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:30.420
I try to think of a concept that is kind of
high-concept where you can tell somebody in

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.080
a minute and they’re like “Oh, that sounds
cool.”

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.500
That’s where I start off when I’m writing.

